     177	\section{Magnetic Auxiliary Variable Shift}
     178	
     179	Define
     180	\[
     181	  a_x=\frac{\sy-\sx}{\epsilon_0},\qquad
     182	  a_y=\frac{\sx-\sy}{\epsilon_0},\qquad
     183	  b_x=\frac{\sx}{\epsilon_0},\qquad
     184	  b_y=\frac{\sy}{\epsilon_0}.
     185	\]
     186	The magnetic equations and \(Q_x,Q_y\) equations suggest the shifted variables
     187	\[
     188	  M_x=Q_x-\mu a_xH_x,\qquad
     189	  M_y=Q_y-\mu a_yH_y.
     190	\]
     191	
     192	\begin{task}[Verify shifted magnetic identities]
     193	Starting from \eqref{eq:main-model}, verify
     194	\begin{equation}
     195	\begin{aligned}
     196	\partial_tM_x
     197	&= a_x\,\partial_yE-\frac{\sy}{\epsilon_0}M_x,\\
     198	\partial_tM_y
     199	&= -a_y\,\partial_xE-\frac{\sx}{\epsilon_0}M_y.
     200	\end{aligned}
     201	\label{eq:magnetic-shift}
     202	\end{equation}
     203	Then test whether an energy written in terms of \(H_x,H_y,M_x,M_y\), rather than \(H_x,H_y,Q_x,Q_y\), has a cleaner dissipation structure.
     204	\end{task}
     205	
     206	\begin{remark}
     207	If \(\sx,\sy\) are spatially variable, \eqref{eq:magnetic-shift} itself does not produce \(\partial_t\sigma\) terms, because the coefficients are time-independent.  However, integrated energy identities may produce \(\partial_x\sx\), \(\partial_y\sy\), or general \(\nabla\sigma_i\) terms after integration by parts.  These terms are likely to be one source of bounded-but-not-monotone estimates.
     208	\end{remark}
     209	
     210	\section{Electric PML Auxiliary Variables}
     211	
     212	The variables \(P_{z,1}\) and \(P_{z,2}\) satisfy
     213	\[
     214	  \partial_tP_{z,1}=-\frac{\epsilon_\infty}{\epsilon_0}\sx\sy E,\qquad
     215	  \partial_tP_{z,2}=-\frac{\sx\sy}{\epsilon_0^2}P.
     216	\]
     217	They appear in the \(E\)-equation as \(E(P_{z,1}+P_{z,2})\).  This resembles the need for time-integrated or cross-term energies in PML analyses.
     218	
     219	\begin{task}[Handle Pz1 and Pz2]
     220	Try the following routes:
     221	\begin{enumerate}
     222	\item define time-integrated variables
     223	\[
     224	  R_1(t)=\int_0^tP_{z,1}(s)\dd s,\qquad
     225	  R_2(t)=\int_0^tP_{z,2}(s)\dd s;
     226	\]
     227	\item add cross terms such as \((E,P_{z,1})\), \((P,P_{z,2})\), or weighted combinations;
     228	\item freeze \(\sx,\sy\) as constants and solve the pointwise Lyapunov matrix problem for the electric subsystem \((E,P,P_{z,1},P_{z,2})\);
     229	\item identify whether the corner factor \(\sx\sy\) is the essential obstruction.
     230	\end{enumerate}
     231	\end{task}
     232	
     233	\section{Baffet-Type Energy Design Clue}
     234	
     235	Baffet--Grote--Imperiale--Kachanovska proved monotone energy decay for a second-order wave-equation PML with constant damping coefficients\cite{baffetGroteImperialeKachanovska2019}.  Their main lesson is methodological: do not start from the naive energy alone.  In two dimensions they introduce
     236	\[
     237	  \lambda=\nabla u+\phi,\qquad
     238	  a=\operatorname{tr}\Gamma_1,\qquad
     239	  b=\det\Gamma_1,
     240	\]
     241	and use an energy of the form
     242	\[
     243	  E[u,\phi]=\frac12\left(
     244	  \|\partial_tu+a u\|^2+\|\nabla u+\phi\|^2
     245	  +\|\phi\|^2_{a^{-1}\Gamma_1}+\|u\|^2_b\right).
     246	\]
     247	In three dimensions they further introduce time-integrated auxiliary variables \(\Phi,\Psi\) and a shifted variable \(q\).
     248	
     249	\begin{task}[Translate the Baffet idea to \eqref{eq:main-model}]
     250	Look for Maxwell--PML analogues of the following structures:
     251	\begin{itemize}
     252	\item shifted field variables analogous to \(\partial_tu+a u\);
     253	\item combinations of principal variables and PML auxiliaries analogous to \(\nabla u+\phi\);
     254	\item time-integrated variables such as \(\int_0^tP_{z,1}\dd s\), \(\int_0^tP_{z,2}\dd s\), \(\int_0^tQ_x\dd s\), \(\int_0^tQ_y\dd s\);
     255	\item cross terms that turn \(E P_{z,1}\), \(E P_{z,2}\), \(H_xQ_x\), \(H_yQ_y\) into exact time derivatives plus nonpositive remainders.
     256	\end{itemize}
     257	Any candidate energy must be checked for positive definiteness and boundary contributions.
     258	\end{task}
     259	
     260	\section{Pointwise Lyapunov Matrix Route}
     261	
     262	For the \(\alpha=1\) case and constant \(\sx,\sy\), use
     263	\[
     264	  \partial_tP=\frac{\chi E-P}{\tau}
     265	\]
     266	to rewrite \eqref{eq:main-model} as a first-order system for
     267	\[
     268	  U=(E,H_x,H_y,P,P_{z,1},P_{z,2},Q_x,Q_y)^T.
     269	\]
     270	After Fourier transform in space,
     271	\[
     272	  \partial_t\widehat U=A(\sx,\sy,k_x,k_y)\widehat U.
     273	\]
     274	Search for a Hermitian positive matrix \(G\) such that
     275	\[
     276	  G A + A^*G\le0.
     277	\]
     278	If such a \(G\) is independent of \(k\), it suggests a local \(L^2\) energy.  If \(G\) depends polynomially on \(k\), it suggests derivative energies.  If no positive \(G\) exists in a natural class, record the obstruction.
     279	
     280	\begin{task}[Algebraic feasibility test]
     281	Perform the pointwise Lyapunov search in the following order:
     282	\begin{enumerate}
     283	\item no PML: \(\sx=\sy=0\);
     284	\item edge PML: \(\sx>0,\sy=0\);
     285	\item edge PML: \(\sx=0,\sy>0\);
     286	\item symmetric corner: \(\sx=\sy>0\);
     287	\item asymmetric corner: \(\sx>0,\sy>0,\sx\ne\sy\).
     288	\end{enumerate}
     289	For each case, record whether there is an explicit positive quadratic energy, whether it is monotone, and whether the shifted variables \(M_x,M_y\) or integrated variables improve the result.
     290	\end{task}
     291	
     292	\section{Fractional Case \texorpdfstring{\(0<\alpha<1\)}{0<alpha<1}}
     293	
     294	The first stage should analyze \(\alpha=1\).  Only after the \(\alpha=1\) mechanism is understood should the fractional case be considered.  For \(0<\alpha<1\), the equation
     295	\[
     296	  P+\tau^\alpha\partial_t^\alpha P=\chi E
     297	\]
     298	requires fractional-memory tools.  A possible route is to use a diffusive representation or complete monotonicity to produce a positive memory energy.
     299	
     300	\begin{task}[Fractional memory extension]
     301	Assuming the \(\alpha=1\) energy has been found or its obstruction understood, determine whether the fractional polarization can be added as a positive memory energy.  Do not assume the correct memory weight; derive it from the Cole--Cole law.  If the fractional part is not needed for the current goal, explicitly postpone it and keep the main theorem for \(\alpha=1\).
     302	\end{task}
     303	
     304	\section{Deliverables Requested from Iteris}
     305	
     306	An Iteris run should aim to produce one of the following terminal artifacts.
     307	
     308	\begin{enumerate}
     309	\item A proved monotone energy identity for \eqref{eq:main-model} with \(\alpha=1\), under clearly stated assumptions.
     310	\item A proved bounded-energy or no-abnormal-growth estimate for \eqref{eq:main-model} with \(\alpha=1\), if monotonicity is unavailable.
     311	\item A verified obstruction showing why a natural class of quadratic energies cannot be monotone for the full two-dimensional corner PML.
     312	\item A staged result: edge PML solved, corner PML reduced to a finite-dimensional Lyapunov condition, fractional \(0<\alpha<1\) case postponed with precise reason.
     313	\end{enumerate}
     314	
     315	Every result should state:
     316	\begin{itemize}
     317	\item exact model equations used;
     318	\item whether \(\alpha=1\) or \(0<\alpha<1\);
     319	\item boundary assumptions, especially how PEC is used;
     320	\item assumptions on \(\sx,\sy\);

